[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Reflection, Annotations & Annotation Processing in Android Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Metaprogramming concepts like annotations, reflection, and annotation processing are essential tools in Android development. They allow developers to write clean, boilerplate-free code by delegating repetitive tasks to compile-time or runtime generators."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding Annotations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Annotations provide metadata about the program to the compiler, runtime environment, or build tools. In Android, they are heavily used in libraries like Room, Dagger, and Retrofit."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining a Custom Annotation"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.FIELD)
public @interface InjectView {
    int value();
}"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@Retention defines how long the annotation is stored. RUNTIME means it remains available during app execution via reflection."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"@Target specifies where the annotation can be applied, such as classes, methods, or fields."},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"2. Runtime Reflection in Android"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection allows inspecting and modifying runtime behavior of classes, interfaces, fields, and methods. While powerful, reflection has performance overhead and should be used sparingly in Android."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Reflection to Process Annotations at Runtime"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public static void bindViews(Object activity) {
    Class<?> clazz = activity.getClass();
    for (java.lang.reflect.Field field : clazz.getDeclaredFields()) {
        if (field.isAnnotationPresent(InjectView.class)) {
            InjectView annotation = field.getAnnotation(InjectView.class);
            int viewId = annotation.value();
            try {
                field.setAccessible(true);
                android.view.View view = ((android.app.Activity) activity).findViewById(viewId);
                field.set(activity, view);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"3. Compile-Time Annotation Processing"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To avoid the performance penalty of runtime reflection, modern Android libraries use Annotation Processing (via AbstractProcessor) to generate Java code at compile time."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Steps to Create a Custom Annotation Processor"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a separate Java library module for the processor."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extend javax.annotation.processing.AbstractProcessor and override the process() method."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use JavaPoet or standard file writing APIs to output new Java source files during compilation."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Sample Annotation Processor Skeleton"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.InjectView")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_8)
public class ViewInjectorProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {
    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(InjectView.class)) {
            // Code generation logic goes here
        }
        return true;
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use runtime reflection for flexibility when performance is not critical, but prefer compile-time annotation processing for high-performance Android applications to maintain fast execution speeds and low memory overhead."}]
